Consider the $GL(\mathbb{R}^2)$ invariant of weight $1$
\begin{align*}
  x_1 x_2 - x_2 x_1.
\end{align*}
Since elements of $GL_0(\mathbb{R}^2)$ leave the variable $x_0$ unchanged,
a straightforward way to produce $GL_0$ invariants presents itself:
insert $x_0$ at the same position in every monomial.
For example
\begin{align*}
  x_1 x_0 x_2 - x_2 x_0 x_1
\end{align*}
is a $GL_0(\mathbb{R}^2)$ invariant of weight $1$.
We now formalize this idea and show that we get every $GL_0$ invariant this way.

